  "query_preview": "..Temperatures across the area remain mild with low\n80s once again for KSLC. Convection is currently ongoing across\nportions of central and northern Utah, primarily in the terrain.\nThese storms are generally weak with high bases and frequent\nlightning. These storms will fizzle out after sunset as they lose\ntheir diurnal forcing. Tomorrow will see some more isolated\nconvection develop similar to today along with increasing\nsoutherly winds as a trough to the northwest starts to gradually \nmove closer. Winds will increase even more on Saturday,\nparticularly across the west desert where there is ~50% chance of\nwind gusts 50+ mph and ~25% chance of gusts 58+ mph.\n\nThe remnants of Tropical Storm Rachel in the eastern Pacific will\nbring widespread precipitation resulting in a flooding threat as\nwell as a severe threat from thunderstorms and strong winds\nassociated with the post tropical circulation. Currently, Tropical\nStorm Rachel is situated ~915 miles west of the southern tip of\nBaja California. The current track of this storm bring Rachel into\nthe northern Baja Saturday night as a tropical storm. This track\nthen continues into the Mojave before moving into southern Utah\nduring the day on Sunday.\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms start to move into the Utah,\nbut the main event holds off until Sunday when the circulation\ncenter of the post-tropical remnants of Rachel move into Utah. The\ncurrent NHC forecast has the mean track of this circulation moving\ninto southern Utah during the day on Sunday before traversing into\nnorthern Utah by Sunday evening. The track of this circulation\nwill be pivotal regarding the details of this forecast. Generally,\nareas along and northwest of this track will see the highest\nprobability of heavy rain, and areas to the southeast will see the\ngreatest potential for gusty winds. Additionally, there could be a\nsevere component Sunday morning across southern Utah as the\ncirculation moves through and discreet convection develops.\nHodographs suggest rotation with the potential for some isolated\nbrief tornados.\n\nOverall rainfall amounts from Rachel are between 1-3\" with some\nlocations, particularly southerly facing mountain aspects of 3\"+.\nRemnants from Rachel will be moving out of the area by Monday, but\nthe closed low to our west will continue to meander across the\nregion through the middle of next week with most scattered showers\nand thunderstorms remaining possible.",
